      "title": "Spacecraft system failures and anomalies attributed to the natural space environment",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Leach R.D."
        }
      ],
      "year": 1995,
      "abstract": "The natural space environment is characterized by man}' complex and subtle phenomena that can be hostile to spacecraft. The effects of these phenomena csv impact spacecraft design, development, and operations. Space systems have become increasingly more susceptible to the space environment as composite materials and smaller, faster electronics have come into greater usage. These current trends make an understanding of the natural spacc environment essential in order to accomplish overall mission objectives. This is especially true in the current climate of better/cheaper/faster. This paper provides a brief overview of the natural space environment: its definition. related programmatic issues, and its effects on various spacecraft subsystems. The primary focus is. however, to document, through representative case histories, spacecraft failures and anomalies that have occurred due to the effects of the natural space environment. This paper had its origin as a report to acquaint NASA management and systems personnel with the importance of natural space environment activities in conjunction with space flight programs.",
